### Tarjan's SCC Algorithm via Low-Link Values

Tarjan's algorithm runs a single DFS, assigning each node a `disc[v]` (discovery time/index) and maintaining a `low[v]` value: the smallest discovery index reachable from `v`'s subtree via tree edges plus at most one back/cross edge to a node still on an active stack. A node is the root of an SCC exactly when `low[v] == disc[v]` — meaning nothing in its subtree can reach further back than itself, so its subtree (up to the stack) forms a complete SCC that can be popped off.

The key trick that avoids a second pass: an auxiliary stack tracks nodes currently "in progress" (on the current DFS path or reachable back to it), and a node only contributes to `low[]` updates of an ancestor if it's *still on that stack* — a cross edge to an already-fully-processed and popped node (which belongs to an already-finalized, different SCC) is ignored. This distinguishes "back edges within the current SCC-in-progress" from "edges to unrelated, already-resolved components," which is exactly the information Kosaraju's algorithm has to recover by doing a second DFS on the transposed graph.

```typescript
function tarjanSCC(graph: Map<number, number[]>): number[][] {
  let index = 0;
  const disc = new Map<number, number>();
  const low = new Map<number, number>();
  const onStack = new Set<number>();
  const stack: number[] = [];
  const sccs: number[][] = [];

  function strongconnect(v: number) {
    disc.set(v, index); low.set(v, index); index++;
    stack.push(v); onStack.add(v);

    for (const w of graph.get(v) ?? []) {
      if (!disc.has(w)) {
        strongconnect(w);
        low.set(v, Math.min(low.get(v)!, low.get(w)!));
      } else if (onStack.has(w)) {
        low.set(v, Math.min(low.get(v)!, disc.get(w)!)); // key: only if w still on stack
      }
    }

    if (low.get(v) === disc.get(v)) {
      const component: number[] = [];
      let w: number;
      do {
        w = stack.pop()!;
        onStack.delete(w);
        component.push(w);
      } while (w !== v);
      sccs.push(component);
    }
  }

  for (const v of graph.keys()) if (!disc.has(v)) strongconnect(v);
  return sccs;
}
```

Kosaraju needs the graph transpose because it separates the two pieces of information Tarjan interleaves: first DFS establishes a finishing-time ordering, second DFS on the reversed graph (processed in decreasing finish order) discovers exactly the SCCs — it needs two full traversals because it can't tell "still in progress" from "finished" without a second graph structure to query.

**Common pitfall:** using `disc[w]` instead of `low[w]` when updating `low[v]` for a tree edge (the recursive call case) — you must propagate the child's `low[w]`, not its `disc[w]`, because the child's subtree might reach back further than the child's own discovery time; using `disc` there silently produces wrong SCC boundaries for any graph with depth > 2.

